  "hints": [
    "90% of traffic is video. Where should it be served from?",
    "Uploads shouldn't block the API: put each upload on a queue for Transcoder workers, which write the results to object storage.",
    "Client \u2192 CDN (2) and Client \u2192 LB (2) \u2192 Video API (5) \u2192 Metadata Cache + NoSQL. API \u2192 Queue \u2192 Transcoders (2) \u2192 S3."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 30,
    "costBudget": 1600
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Video is streamed as small segments (HLS/DASH) so the CDN can cache them and players can switch resolution mid-stream.",
    "Transcoding is slow and CPU-heavy, so it runs as background jobs. The upload request just stores the original and enqueues work.",
    "Object storage is the durable home for every rendition. The CDN pulls segments from it on a cache miss.",
    "The API itself is small and read-heavy: a metadata cache and a NoSQL store handle titles, views and recommendations."
  ]
}
